  "body": "## State found (2026-11-04, verified on amd-6)\n\n`loginctl show-user user -p Linger` returns **Linger=yes** (and `/var/lib/systemd/linger/user` exists), so systemd user services on the laptop already start at boot without a login. But that state is **imperative** — set by hand (believed around 2026-10-23, for RustDesk) — and is not declared anywhere in this repo. A reinstall or a config rebuild from a tree that does not know about it would silently lose it, and the failure mode (user services simply not running until someone logs in) is exactly the kind that goes unnoticed.\n\n## Ask\n\nDeclare it in the laptop's NixOS configuration in this repo:\n\n```nix\nusers.users.<name>.linger = true;\n```\n\nThis should ride the **next natural config change** — no dedicated switch just for this. While the imperative state holds, nothing is broken.\n\n## Boundary worth recording\n\nLinger starts the user manager at boot; it does **not** unlock the GNOME keyring. Keyring-held credentials still require a graphical login — observed 2026-11-04: after the 05:47 reboot, the laptop `gh` CLI credential was dead until a desktop login, even with linger on. Fleet services must keep using file-based secrets (sops / Bitwarden), never the keyring, so they are boot-independent.\n",
